          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for treating an acute migraine, and when is prevention considered?",
          "answer": "Guidance for treating acute migraine states that treatment can be given orally, or by non-oral routes if vomiting or problems with stomach emptying make oral treatment unreliable. An acute treatment should be tried for two or three separate attacks before deciding if it is ineffective, as individual attacks can vary greatly (National headache society guidance).\n\nPreventive measures for migraine are considered when acute treatment alone is not enough, or if attacks are frequent or disabling. This generally applies when there are four or more migraine days per month with some disability. Prevention may also be considered for fewer attacks if the disability is severe, if acute treatments don't work or cannot be used, or if a person prefers preventive treatment (National headache society guidance). The goal of prevention is to reduce the frequency and severity of attacks, with a 50% reduction in migraine days considered a successful outcome (National headache society guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
